steps.body:
Most of an API's traffic is reads: people look far more often than they change anything. And every program between your API and its clients, from browsers to caches, treats a GET as harmless. They repeat it, prefetch it, and keep copies of it. So the rules for reads are the ones you can least afford to break.

## Read one, read many

Roombook has two reads for rooms. `GET /v1/rooms/{roomID}` answers **200 OK** with one room, or 404. `GET /v1/rooms` answers 200 with every room, in `internal/api/rooms.go`:

```go
func listRooms(rooms *memory.RoomStore) http.HandlerFunc {
	return func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		writeJSON(w, http.StatusOK, map[string]any{"items": rooms.List()})
	}
}
```

The list is wrapped in an object, `{"items": [...]}`, instead of being sent as a bare array. That leaves room to add fields next to it later without breaking anyone: the next lesson adds `nextCursor`, for reading a long list a page at a time. A bare array can't grow that way. An empty list is still a success: 200 with `{"items": []}`, never 404, because the list exists, it just has nothing in it yet.

Here's the handler for one room, with the helper every room route uses:

```go
func getRoom(rooms *memory.RoomStore) http.HandlerFunc {
	return func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		room, ok := findRoom(w, r, rooms)
		if !ok {
			return
		}
		writeJSON(w, http.StatusOK, room)
	}
}

// findRoom reads the room named in the path, or answers 404 and reports false.
func findRoom(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request, rooms *memory.RoomStore) (domain.Room, bool) {
	id, ok := parseID(r.PathValue("roomID"))
	if !ok {
		writeError(w, http.StatusNotFound, "Room not found")
		return domain.Room{}, false
	}
	room, ok := rooms.Get(id)
	if !ok {
		writeError(w, http.StatusNotFound, "Room not found")
		return domain.Room{}, false
	}
	return room, true
}
```

The `return` after `findRoom` fails matters. Without it, the handler would carry on and write a second answer. Go doesn't stop you: it logs `http: superfluous response.WriteHeader call` on the server, and the client gets the error with a room glued on after it. Every path through a handler should write exactly one response.

## Where the rooms live, for now

`internal/store/memory/rooms.go` keeps the rooms in memory, in a map from id to room, filled with Roombook's three rooms when the server starts. Two things in it are Go-specific:

```go
func (s *RoomStore) List() []domain.Room {
	s.mu.Lock()
	defer s.mu.Unlock()
	list := make([]domain.Room, 0, len(s.rooms))
	for _, room := range s.rooms {
		list = append(list, room)
	}
	sort.Slice(list, func(i, j int) bool { return list[i].ID < list[j].ID })
	return list
}
```

- **A lock.** Requests run in goroutines, at the same time, so two of them could use the map at once. `s.mu` is a `sync.Mutex`: `Lock` lets one goroutine in at a time, and `defer s.mu.Unlock()` lets the next one in when the method returns. Every method of the store does the same.
- **Sorting.** Go visits a map's entries in a random order, on purpose, so code can't depend on it. `sort.Slice` puts the rooms in id order, so the list is the same every time.

The room type, `domain.Room`, lives in `internal/domain`, with JSON tags like `json:"building"` that name its fields in the JSON. Memory forgets everything when the server stops, so your changes vanish on every restart. That's fine while you learn routing. The lesson on services and repositories explains why stores are a separate layer, and the PostgreSQL lesson swaps this one for a database without touching the routes.

## Safe means no changes

Recall from the web course that GET is **safe**: it only reads. For an API, this is a promise other programs rely on. A chat app fetches every link someone pastes to show a preview. A browser may load links before you click them, to be quick. A client retries a GET that timed out. If `GET /v1/bookings/1001/cancel` cancelled a booking, pasting that link in a group chat would cancel it. So a GET never changes anything a client can see. Writing a log line, or counting visits, is fine.

## HEAD

**HEAD** asks for exactly what GET would answer, without the body: the same status and the same headers. A client uses it to check that something exists, or how big it is, without downloading it. Go's router sends HEAD requests to your GET handler and throws the body away, so you get it for free.

A read answers 200 with the thing, or 404 when it isn't there. Later lessons add 304 Not Modified, when the client's copy is still current, in the next lesson, and 403 or 404 for something the caller isn't allowed to see, in the lesson on permissions.
